From: ysfaculty <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 09:12:07 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 165/167] hjviewer: the portable packer compresses its files on
 every core

generate.py compressed each file of the Windows app with Brotli at
quality 11 one after another, which took the Windows job some three
minutes of one core while the runner's other core waited. It now walks
the folder as before, printing the same lines in the same order, and then
compresses the files in worker processes, the largest first so that no
big file starts last. Each file is still compressed whole by the same
call, and the table keeps the walk's order, so data.bin (and a --package
blob) is the same byte for byte. The worker is a top-level function and
the entry point stays behind its __main__ guard, as Windows' spawned
workers need. A folder of one file, or a machine with one core, compresses
in the process as before. The command line does not change.

On a test folder of 12 files (8 MB of binaries, fonts, text, random bytes,
an empty file, nested folders and a name with a space) the old and the new
script wrote the same data.bin from the workflow's command line, under
fork and under spawn, and the same blob with --package --exclude-exe; on
two cores the new one took 6.2 s for the old one's 11.8 s.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
